<div class="wrap">

<h1>postmarketOS,<br>two Xiaomis
<small>xiaomi-surya · xiaomi-vayu</small></h1>

<p class="lede">A signed apk repository and a flashable image, so a clean
postmarketOS install becomes a working phone with one <code>apk add</code> — and
stays current with <code>apk upgrade</code>.</p>

<h3>The short version</h3>
<pre>sudo wget -qO /etc/apk/keys/@KEY@ @URL@/keys/@KEY@
echo @URL@/main | sudo tee -a /etc/apk/repositories
sudo apk update &amp;&amp; sudo apk add utsugi-surya-full
<b># then reboot, deliberately — see the warning under Install</b></pre>

<p>Prefer not to install anything? There is a ready image on the
<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/releases">releases page</a>.</p>

<h2 id="devices">Devices &amp; what does not work</h2>

<p>Two phones, two SoCs, nothing shared between them: not a firmware, not a
config, not a patch.</p>

<div class="grid">
	<div><b>POCO X3 NFC</b><span>SM7150 · <code>xiaomi-surya</code><br>
	Usable as a daily phone. Modem, cameras, voice, sensors and GPS all work.</span></div>
	<div><b>POCO X3 Pro</b><span>SM8150 · <code>xiaomi-vayu</code><br>
	<b style="display:inline">Experimental.</b> The modem does not work, so it is
	not a phone. Published for whoever wants to continue the investigation.</span></div>
</div>

<h3>Surya — not supported</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Hardware</th><th>State</th></tr>
<tr class="no"><td>Fingerprint</td><td><b>No path.</b> All four routes tried: MMIO to the
controller hangs, MMIO to the pin panics, <code>spi-gpio</code> does not boot, and the
TrustZone does not cover the TLMM.</td></tr>
<tr class="no"><td>Stereo audio</td><td><b>Not possible without kernel work.</b> The I2S bus
carries only the left channel, so both speakers are fed the same mix. Splitting them by
channel leaves the bottom one mute.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Surya — known issues</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Issue</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr><td>Echo in calls</td><td>Voice is heard on both sides, with echo. The canceller is
wired and tunable at runtime; it is not yet dialled in.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Microphone</td><td>There is signal in a recording, but that it is the microphone
and not noise is unconfirmed.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shutdown freeze</td><td>The phone powers off correctly, but <b>why</b> the teardown
freezes is still unexplained. The fix hands the power-off to the kernel before reaching
that point rather than curing it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>GPS engine lock</td><td>pmaports leaves the location engine locked on every
Qualcomm device. Unlocked here; the bug is upstream.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Vayu — known issues</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Issue</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr class="no"><td>Modem</td><td><b>Dies 2 to 4 s after the radio is switched on.</b>
Pinned down to a single firmware assertion and <b>not fixed</b>. Without a modem it is
not a usable phone.</td></tr>
<tr><td>IPA firmware</td><td>Proprietary, so it is not shipped. A tool copies it off your
own stock partition: <code>vayu-ipa-firmware-extract</code>.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="features">What you get</h2>

<h3>Kernel — 78 patches</h3>
<table>
<tr><td>Cameras</td><td>All three, including C-PHY support in CAMSS and three sensor
drivers written for this phone. Rear autofocus runs as a user-space engine against a
measured curve.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Voice in call</td><td>Q6VOICE brought up: topologies registered from the stock
ACDB, the vocproc modules turned on, the transmit path unmuted.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Volume</td><td>The amplifier driver wrote the coefficient least-significant byte
first; the chip latches on the low byte, so it stored garbage and the phone booted
mute.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Panel</td><td>60 Hz as well as 120, which is what makes a power-saving mode
worth having.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Power</td><td>A power-off that powers off, a charge screen when you plug in a
phone that is off, and a watchdog that removes the need for a person when it hangs.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>System</h3>
<p>What used to be an installer writing into <code>/usr/local</code>, <code>/etc</code>
and <code>$HOME</code> over ssh is now packages: helpers in <code>/usr/libexec</code>,
units enabled by systemd presets, overrides as drop-ins instead of overwritten files, and
Plasma defaults in <code>/etc/xdg</code> — the layer under <code>$HOME</code>, so anything
you change still wins.</p>

<h2 id="apps">Applications</h2>
<p>Six, written for this phone because Plasma Mobile does not ship them. Each lives in its
own repository and is built here from a tagged tarball.</p>

<div class="grid">
	<div><b>poconav</b><span>Car navigation with neural-network voice, made to be read
	from a dashboard.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/poconav">poconav →</a></div>
	<div><b>screenglaze</b><span>Screenshots with volume-down + power, like Android, and a
	sheet to share, save or crop.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/screenglaze">screenglaze →</a></div>
	<div><b>keepalive</b><span>Apps you mark minimise instead of closing when you swipe
	their card away, so the next open is instant.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/keepalive">keepalive →</a></div>
	<div><b>app-usage</b><span>Battery use per application — the screen Plasma Mobile does
	not have.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/app-usage">app-usage →</a></div>
	<div><b>smart-unlock</b><span>Skip the PIN on a trusted wifi, for a while after
	unlocking, or in chosen time slots.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/smart-unlock">smart-unlock →</a></div>
	<div><b>lost-phone</b><span>Find your phone without Google: SMS, your own network, your
	own relay, or ntfy.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/lost-phone">lost-phone →</a></div>
</div>

<h2 id="install">Installation</h2>

<p>postmarketOS is Alpine-based, so this is <b>apk</b>, not apt.</p>

<h3>1 · On an existing install</h3>
<pre>sudo wget -qO /etc/apk/keys/@KEY@ @URL@/keys/@KEY@
echo @URL@/main | sudo tee -a /etc/apk/repositories
sudo apk update
sudo apk add utsugi-surya-full</pre>

<table>
<tr><th>Package</th><th>What it brings</th></tr>
<tr><td>utsugi-surya-base</td><td>The hardware: patched kernel, audio, sensors, GPS,
cameras, power-off, charge mode.</td></tr>
<tr><td>utsugi-surya-full</td><td>Base, plus the six applications and the Plasma Mobile
fixes.</td></tr>
<tr><td>utsugi-surya-extras</td><td>Optional applications: browser, mail, maps, messaging,
photos. A choice, not a fix, which is why it is separate.</td></tr>
<tr><td>utsugi-vayu-base</td><td>The experimental POCO X3 Pro set.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="warn"><b>Reboot on purpose, not by habit</b>
The kernel comes with <code>base</code>. On the surya a kernel that does not boot is not a
failed install, it is a reboot loop: the watchdog fires 20 s after PID 1 and there is no
screen left to fix it from. And the <code>boot</code> partition holds U-Boot, not the
kernel — writing a kernel there leaves the phone unable to boot at all. Read
<a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/blob/main/flash/surya.md">flash/surya.md</a>
before the first one.</div>

<h3>2 · Flash a ready image</h3>
<p>On the <a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports/releases">releases
page</a>, with everything already inside. It carries the repository line and the signing
key, so <code>apk upgrade</code> tracks updates from the first boot.</p>
<pre>sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
xz -d utsugi-surya-&lt;tag&gt;.img.xz
fastboot flash userdata utsugi-surya-&lt;tag&gt;.img   <b># userdata. never boot.</b></pre>

<h3>3 · Encrypted — you build it</h3>
<p>There is no encrypted image to download, and that is on purpose. A LUKS master key is
created when the disk is formatted, which for an image means on the machine that builds
it. Changing the passphrase later replaces the slot protecting that key, <b>not the
key</b>. An encrypted image handed out would give whoever built it the master key of every
phone flashed from it.</p>
<pre>scripts/build-your-own-image --encrypted</pre>
<p>pmbootstrap asks <b>you</b> for the passphrase and the key is generated on your machine.
Tested end to end on the surya: the initramfs opens a LUKS that lives inside a loop file on
<code>userdata</code>, and <code>unl0kr</code> draws its keyboard on this panel.</p>

<h2 id="contribute">Contribute</h2>

<p>Everything is here. The packaging is MIT; each patch and application keeps the licence
of what it patches.</p>

<h3>Repositories</h3>
<div class="grid">
	<div><b>utsugi-pmaports</b><span>The packages, the kernel patches, the build and publish
	scripts.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/utsugi-pmaports">utsugi-pmaports →</a></div>
	<div><b>poconav</b><span>Car navigation.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/poconav">poconav →</a></div>
	<div><b>screenglaze</b><span>Screenshots.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/screenglaze">screenglaze →</a></div>
	<div><b>keepalive</b><span>Apps that stay alive.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/keepalive">keepalive →</a></div>
	<div><b>app-usage</b><span>Battery per application.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/app-usage">app-usage →</a></div>
	<div><b>smart-unlock</b><span>Skip the PIN when it is safe.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/smart-unlock">smart-unlock →</a></div>
	<div><b>lost-phone</b><span>Find the phone without Google.</span><br><a href="https://github.com/utsugi-pmos/lost-phone">lost-phone →</a></div>
</div>

<h3>Building it</h3>
<pre>scripts/setup-workdir   <b># separate pmbootstrap work dir, pinned pmaports, signing key</b>
scripts/build --all     <b># the kernel takes minutes</b>
scripts/lint            <b># fork bookkeeping and version bumps</b>
scripts/check-upstream  <b># says when a fork has fallen behind</b>
scripts/publish --push  <b># sign and publish</b></pre>

<h3>Where help is most useful</h3>
<table>
<tr><td>Fingerprint</td><td>Four routes are documented as dead ends. A fifth would be
welcome.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Call echo</td><td>The canceller is wired and tunable at runtime. It needs someone
with patience and a second phone.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vayu modem</td><td>The fault is pinned to one firmware assertion. Everything
measured is written down.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upstreaming</td><td>Several fixes are not device-specific — the GPS engine lock
affects every Qualcomm device on postmarketOS.</td></tr>
</table>

